%[text] # USV Draft: A Well-Formatted Live Script
%[text] This is an illustrative example for class, not a student submission. It solves the Assignment 4 USV draft exercise and uses the live script's features to explain the work: a title, a heading for each step, equations written as math, a figure, and short text before each block of code saying what the code does and why.
%[text] We estimate the draft $d$ of the WAM-V 16 USV, the depth of its hulls below the waterline:
%[text] ![](text:image:a4d1)
%%
%[text] ## Model
%[text] Each of the two hulls is modeled as a cylinder of radius $R = 0.213$ m and length $L = 5$ m. The submerged volume of one hull at draft $d$ is
%[text] $V(d) = L \\left( R^2 \\arccos\\left(1 - \\frac{d}{R}\\right) - (R - d)\\sqrt{R^2 - (R - d)^2} \\right)$
%[text] At equilibrium the mass of water displaced by both hulls equals the vessel mass, $m = 200$ kg, with water density $\\rho = 1000$ kg/m$^3$:
%[text] $2 \\rho V(d) = m$
%[text] Moving everything to one side turns this into a root-finding problem. The draft we want is the root of
%[text] $f(d) = 2 \\rho V(d) - m = 0$
%%
%[text] ## Local function
%[text] `usv_balance` implements $f(d)$, with the parameters defined inside it. It uses element-wise operators so that it also accepts a vector of drafts, which the plot below needs.
function f = usv_balance(d)
    % Parameters
    R = 0.213;          % Hull radius (m)
    L = 5;              % Hull length (m)
    m = 200;            % Vessel mass (kg)
    rho = 1000;         % Density of water (kg/m^3)
    % Submerged volume of one hull (m^3)
    V = L .* (R.^2 .* acos(1 - d./R) - (R - d) .* sqrt(R.^2 - (R - d).^2));
    % Mass of water displaced by both hulls minus the vessel mass (kg)
    f = 2 .* rho .* V - m;
end
%%
%[text] ## Test the function
%[text] With no draft nothing is displaced, so $f(0)$ should be $-m = -200$ kg. With both hulls fully submerged, $d = 2R$, the hulls displace far more than 200 kg, so $f$ should be positive. A sign change between the two means there is a root in between.
R = 0.213;     % Hull radius (m), for the valid range 0 to 2R
f_empty = usv_balance(0)
f_submerged = usv_balance(2*R)
%%
%[text] ## Solve with fzero
%[text] `fzero` takes a function handle to `usv_balance` and an initial estimate. The model only applies for $0 \\le d \\le 2R$, so we start inside that range.
d_guess = 0.1;                        % Initial estimate (m)
d_draft = fzero(@usv_balance, d_guess)
%[text] The estimated draft is about 0.084 m, or 8.4 cm.
%%
%[text] ## Check graphically
%[text] Plot $f(d)$ over the valid range. The `fzero` solution should sit where the curve crosses zero.
dd = linspace(0, 2*R, 200);           % Drafts over the valid range (m)
figure
plot(dd, usv_balance(dd), 'b-')
hold on
yline(0, 'k--')
plot(d_draft, 0, 'ro', 'MarkerFaceColor', 'r')
hold off
grid on
xlabel('Draft, d (m)')
ylabel('Displaced mass minus vessel mass, f(d) (kg)')
title('WAM-V 16 buoyancy balance')
legend('f(d)', 'f = 0', 'fzero solution', 'Location', 'northwest')
%[text] The curve crosses zero at the solution, so the draft is consistent with the model.

